# Architecture

## Overview

A rule catalog reaches a target project two ways. An installer writes each rule into the project, and a sync pass compares an installed copy against the catalog and reports what diverged.

## Key technical decisions

### Rules install by copy rather than by reference

A rule lands in the target as a real file the project edits and owns, chosen over a pointer into the catalog. The installer at `src/gov/install.ts` carries 4 rules today, which is small enough that copying costs nothing and a pointer would cost every project a resolver. Measured at a1b2c3d4 on 2026-07-20.

### Drift reports rather than overwrites

`src/gov/sync.ts` compares an installed copy against the catalog and reports what diverged, chosen over rewriting the file. Overwriting keeps the target uniform and discards the local edits a project made deliberately, and those edits are the reason install copies in the first place.

### The planner runs ahead of the installer

`src/gov/plan.ts` resolves which rules a stack pulls in before anything is written, chosen over resolving inside the writer. The planner reaches 3 stacks and a single pass over them is cheap, while a writer that resolves as it goes cannot report what it is about to do. Measured at a1b2c3d4 on 2026-07-20.

### The toolkit states its rules in one file

`CLAUDE.md` carries 2 standing rules today, chosen over a folder of path-scoped files. That is few enough for a reader to take in one pass, and splitting them would cost more to find than to read. Measured at a1b2c3d4 on 2026-07-20.

## Risks / open questions

- Nothing re-reads a rule count once a stack changes shape.
